{
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD000004.pub2",
  "title": "Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre\u2010eclampsia - Hofmeyr, GJ - 2012 | Cochrane Library",
  "content": "Title: Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre\u2011eclampsia\nAuthor: G. Justus Hofmeyr\nDOI: 10.1002/14651858.CD000004.pub2\nVersion published: 13 June 2012\nSearch date: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group's Trials Register, 2 February 2012\n\nObjective\n- Assess effects of antenatal abdominal decompression for maternal hypertension or impaired fetal growth on perinatal outcome.\n\nSelection criteria and methods\n- Randomised or quasi\u2011randomised trials comparing abdominal decompression versus no decompression in women with pre\u2011eclampsia and/or fetuses considered compromised.\n- Eligibility and trial quality assessed by one review author.\n\nIncluded studies\n- Three studies (reported 1967\u20131973), total N = 356 pregnant women.\n- Study populations: one trial included women with pre\u2011eclampsia, essential hypertension, or chronic nephritis; two trials enrolled pregnancies with fetuses small for gestational age.\n- All studies had the possibility of serious bias (methodological limitations).\n\nIntervention details (reported)\n- Rigid covered dome applied to abdomen; decompression to \u221250 to \u2212100 mm Hg for 15\u201330 seconds each minute for 30 minutes once to thrice daily, or continuously during labour.\n\nMain results (therapeutic abdominal decompression vs no decompression)\n- Persistent pre\u2011eclampsia: RR 0.36, 95% CI 0.18 to 0.72.\n- Fetal distress in labour: RR 0.37, 95% CI 0.19 to 0.71.\n- Low birthweight: RR 0.50, 95% CI 0.40 to 0.63.\n- Apgar score < 6 at 1 minute: RR 0.26, 95% CI 0.12 to 0.56.\n- Perinatal mortality: RR 0.39, 95% CI 0.22 to 0.71.\n\nAuthors' conclusions\n- Because of methodological limitations and potential bias in the included trials, the effects of therapeutic abdominal decompression are not clear.\n- Apparent improvements in birthweight and perinatal mortality are notable and warrant further evaluation in methodologically sound controlled trials for impaired fetal growth and possibly in women with pre\u2011eclampsia.\n- Current evidence does not support routine clinical use.\n\nOther notes\n- Plain language summary reiterates device settings and trial limitations; recommends further rigorous study before clinical adoption.\n- Full text views: 4,983; altmetric score: 3.",
