        "text": "delivered on average two weeks earlier than pregnancies with SGA alone. This is unlikely to explain differences since even with early onset preeclampsia, absorptive surfaces and placental volumes were similar to those of placentas from pregnancies with growth restriction without preeclampsia and less than in normal pregnancy [7]. Thus, the striking differences in system A amino acid transporter activity, normal in preeclamptic pregnancies even when associated with SGA, does not appear to be explained by currently available placental morphological studies including quantitative morpholometric studies. The reduced system A amino acid uptake in placentas from growthrestricted fetuses (without preeclampsia) is also not secondary to reduced viability as LDH release was similar in all diagnostic groups.\n\nThe birth weight centile for SGA infants with and without preeclampsia were nearly identical (5.7 \u00b1 0.7 vs. 5.9 \u00b1 0.8) as were placental weight and infant/placental weight ratios ( Table 1). Given that the placentas of SGA infants without preeclampsia had significantly reduced system A amino acid uptake, as has been previously reported [13,14], it is likely that most of these infants were pathologically, not constitutionally, small. Interestingly, in reviewing clinical criteria, we found that objective evidence of IUGR, abnormal Doppler velocimeitry or asymmetric growth profile, was present in 3 of 5 of the SGA infants of preeclamptic mothers and only 1 of 5 of the SGA infants whose mothers did not have preeclampsia. Thus, the normal amino acid uptake by the placentas of SGA infants of preeclamptic mothers was probably not due to less severe growth restriction in these infants.\n\nThis observation that growth restriction with preeclampsia is associated with normal system A amino acid transporter activity while uptake is reduced with SGA alone suggests that growth restriction in the two settings may be substantially different. Several of the me