      "snippet": {
        "text": "In this study we report that system A amino acid transporter activity is not reduced in placentas of SGA infants from preeclamptic pregnancies. We confirm the reduced system A transporter activity previously reported with IUGR pregnancies without preeclampsia [13,14]. Although most prior studies of IUGR without preeclampsia have been with isolated microvillous membrane vesicles, we confirm the same findings in intact placental fragments. In studies of protein restricted rats treated with a regimen that leads to IUGR, system A-mediated amino acid uptake was similarly reduced and preceded reduction in fetal weight suggesting that the change is causally relevant [17].\n\nThe finding of uptake in preeclampsia with normally grown infants equivalent to uptake in uncomplicated pregnancies with normally grown infants is compatible with the relatively normal morphology and absorptive surfaces in these placentas reported previously [7]. Morphological difference between placentas from uncomplicated pregnancies and preeclamptic pregnancies with normally grown fetuses delivered near term (as was the case with the patients in this study) are at most minor. However, the findings of normal amino acid uptake even in placentas of SGA fetuses of preeclamptic pregnancies would not have been predicted by morphological studies. In these studies, abnormalities in placentas from preeclamptic pregnancies with growth restricted infants closely resemble those of growthrestricted fetuses whose mothers do not have preeclampsia [3][4][5][6][7]. In pregnancies complicated by growth restriction with or without preeclampsia obtained after 34 weeks of gestation, absorptive surface and placental volume are similarly reduced below those of uncomplicated pregnancies. Although not statistically significantly different, the pregnancies with SGA infants and preeclampsia in our study delivered on average two weeks earlier than pregnancies with SGA alone. This is unlikely to explain differe