2026-07-26 12:38:25,510 - sciconharness.mcp_client.utils.utils - INFO - Full Results:
{
  "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK577930/",
  "title": "Evidence review for interventions for pre-eclampsia",
  "content": "Summary \u2014 interventions for women with pre-eclampsia (NICE evidence review; literature searched to 07/02/2018)\n\nDefinitions\n- Pre-eclampsia: new hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria or maternal organ dysfunction (renal, hepatic, neurological, haematological).  \n- Severe pre-eclampsia: BP \u2265160/110 mmHg or worsening organ dysfunction (eg HELLP).  \n- Early-onset pre-eclampsia: onset before 34+0 weeks.  \n- Hypertension severity used for subgrouping in the review: mild <149/99 mmHg; moderate 150/100\u2013159/109 mmHg; severe \u2265160/110 mmHg.\n\nScope, methods and evidence base\n- Review followed NICE methods (Developing NICE guidelines: the manual, 2014). Search date last run 07/02/2018. No economic evidence identified; no economic modelling for this question.\n- Included: 1 Cochrane systematic review (Churchill 2013; 4 RCTs, n=425), 18 additional RCTs and 1 retrospective cohort (total RCTs n\u22482,797; cohort n=365). Some trials included mixed hypertensive disorders. Evidence was available for all interventions except statins, abdominal decompression, \u201ctight vs less-tight\u201d management.\n- Quality of evidence across outcomes: ranged from very low to moderate (main risks: unclear randomisation/allocation concealment, lack of blinding). Retrospective cohort likely subject to selection bias and related to women with chronic hypertension with superimposed pre-eclampsia.\n\nKey results by comparison (main outcomes and evidence grades)\n- Labetalol vs nicardipine (acute): one RCT (n=60, low quality) \u2014 no clinically important difference in time to achieve BP control.\n- Labetalol vs no intervention (non-acute): one RCT (n=191)  \n  - Stillbirth: no events in either arm (moderate quality).  \n  - Neonatal death: no difference (very low quality).  \n  - Small-for-gestational-age (SGA): possible increase with labetalol (RR 2.06, 95% CI 0.98\u20134.36) but estimate uncertain (low quality).  \n  - Labetalol reduced episodes of severe hypertension versus no intervention (low quality).\n- Labetalol vs methyldopa (acute): one RCT (n=180, very low quality) \u2014 labetalol reduced mean arterial pressure compared with methyldopa; no difference in induction rates.\n- Hydralazine vs nifedipine (acute): small trials (various; examples n=37 to n=184) \u2014 generally no clinically important differences in neonatal mortality, stillbirth, SGA, birthweight, gestational age, neonatal admissions (evidence grades very low to moderate). Subgroup findings: in some subgroups (eg GA 34+0\u201336+6, severe hypertension, low/middle income setting) nifedipine had faster BP control; in some (<34 weeks, severe hypertension, high-income setting) nifedipine reduced occurrence of severe hypertension.\n- Hydralazine vs labetalol (acute): trials (eg n=200) \u2014 no clinically important differences in stillbirth, neonatal death, birthweight, neonatal admission, severe hypertension, eclampsia, HELLP, placental abruption, mode of birth, or maternal death (evidence very low to moderate).\n- Nifedipine vs labetalol (acute): small RCTs (n~59\u2013109) \u2014 nifedipine achieved faster BP control (clinically important reduction in time to control; very low quality); no detected differences in neonatal mortality, birthweight, gestational age, neonatal admission, HELLP, or eclampsia.\n- Nifedipine vs no intervention (non-acute): one RCT (n=200)  \n  - Stillbirth and neonatal death: no events in either arm (moderate quality).  \n  - Preterm birth (<37 weeks): clinically important increase with nifedipine vs no intervention (moderate quality).  \n  - No clinically important differences in SGA, neonatal admissions, gestational age at birth (grades very low\u2013moderate depending on outcome).\n- Methyldopa vs no intervention (non-acute): one RCT (n=70)  \n  - Perinatal mortality: no difference (very low quality).  \n  - Methyldopa reduced systolic BP vs no treatment but not diastolic (very low quality). No differences in eclampsia or C-section rates.\n- Immediate birth (planned early birth) vs expectant management (several RCTs; 5 RCTs n=700 for perinatal outcomes; subgroup syntheses performed):\n  - Overall stillbirth and neonatal death: no clinically important differences (very low quality).  \n  - SGA: overall no difference (very low quality), but subgroup: for gestational age <34 weeks (3 RCTs, n=400) immediate birth reduced SGA (very low quality). For 34+0\u201336+6 weeks (HYPITAT-II etc) no consistent SGA benefit; immediate birth associated with lower gestational age at birth (ie more earlier births) (4 RCTs, n=425; very low quality).  \n  - Birth weight: in 34+0\u201336+6 weeks one trial (n=169) showed higher birthweight with immediate birth (very low quality).  \n  - Admissions to neonatal unit: overall no difference (very low quality), though in high-income setting some trials found fewer neonatal admissions with expectant management.  \n  - Placental abruption: limited evidence suggests possible reduction with immediate birth (<34 weeks and some severe-hypertension subgroups) but estimates uncertain (eg RR 0.42, 95% CI 0.18\u20131.00).  \n  - No differences in eclampsia, HELLP, maternal death or mode of birth (evidence very low\u2013low).\n- Outpatient vs inpatient management: one retrospective observational multicentre study (Schoen 2017, n=365; chronic hypertension with superimposed pre-eclampsia) \u2014 outpatient care associated with fewer SGA births, higher birthweights and longer gestation (low to very low quality), but likely subject to selection bias (physician discretion for admission); no difference in stillbirth or neonatal admission. Evidence not robust enough to change practice; results limited to chronic hypertension with superimposed pre-eclampsia.\n- Other: no evidence identified for statins, abdominal decompression, or trials directly comparing tight vs less-tight BP management in pre-eclampsia within this scope. Subgroup analyses by gestational age, hypertension severity and country income were performed where data allowed.\n\nCommittee interpretation, conclusions and recommendations\n- Outcomes judged most critical: maternal BP control; perinatal mortality (stillbirth, neonatal death); small-for-gestational-age. Important secondary outcomes included birthweight, gestational age, preterm birth, neonatal unit admission, neurodevelopmental and neurosensory outcomes, and maternal outcomes (eclampsia, HELLP, placental abruption, mode of birth, maternal death).\n- Evidence quality was generally low or very low; biases noted. Where evidence existed it often showed no clinically important differences between commonly used antihypertensives for most neonatal outcomes.\n- Antihypertensive drug choice: insufficient RCT evidence to recommend one agent over another based solely on efficacy/safety in pre-eclampsia. Committee adopted prior guideline approach:\n  - Labetalol recommended first-line (licensed in pregnancy).  \n  - Nifedipine and methyldopa as alternatives (methyldopa third-line based on broader Cochrane evidence for hypertensive pregnancy).  \n  - Note: some evidence labetalol may increase SGA (uncertain), and beta-blockers can cause neonatal hypoglycaemia.\n  - Intravenous hydralazine appropriate in acute settings but not for routine ongoing antenatal hypertension management.\n- Speed of BP reduction: oral nifedipine often produced more rapid BP lowering than IV labetalol in some trials; clinical benefit/harm of rapid lowering is unclear (concern of reduced fetal blood flow).\n- BP treatment thresholds and targets (adopted from CHIPS and adult hypertension guidance):\n  - Treatment initiation threshold: diastolic \u226590 mmHg (CHIPS/Magee 2015) and systolic \u2265140 mmHg (aligned with adult hypertension guideline).  \n  - Target BP: \u226485 mmHg diastolic (CHIPS) and \u2264135 mmHg systolic (adult guideline).\n- Monitoring and place of care:\n  - If not admitted, BP monitoring changed to at least every 48 hours (more frequently if inpatient). Dipstick proteinuria testing to continue only if clinical change or diagnostic uncertainty. Fetal growth monitoring frequency included in management table (refer to guideline section 1.6).\n  - fullPIERS and PREP-S risk prediction models are useful to stratify risk. Committee recommends considering admission for surveillance if fullPIERS risk \u226530%, but models should not be used in isolation; clinical judgment remains essential (admit regardless of risk score for SBP \u2265160 mmHg, concerning lab tests, clinical signs, or fetal compromise). This recommendation was partly informed by a separate cost-effectiveness model suggesting a 30% threshold may be cost-effective compared with universal inpatient care (uncertainty remains).\n- Timing of birth:\n  - No major change to prior timing recommendations overall, but committee extended the conservative management cutoff from 34 to 37 weeks for women with severe hypertension only if none of the identified \u2018red flag\u2019 features are present (based on HYPITAT II trial data), to benefit neonatal outcomes by prolonging pregnancy to 37 weeks where safe.\n- Research recommendation:\n  - Priority research question: In which women with pre-eclampsia is inpatient management associated with better outcomes for mothers and babies? (need to determine appropriate place-of-care criteria and validate implications of adopting a fullPIERS/PREP-S 30% threshold).\n- Other notes:\n  - PHOENIX trial (investigating timing of birth for late preterm pre-eclampsia, 34+0\u201336+6 weeks) noted as forthcoming and may provide further data.\n  - Review excluded broader Cochrane analyses that included mixed hypertensive populations; committee considered Cochrane findings (beta-blockers and calcium-channel blockers more effective than methyldopa to prevent severe hypertension) when positioning methyldopa as third-line.\n\nSelected referenced trials and resources (examples cited in the review)\n- CHIPS (Magee 2015) informed BP targets and initiation thresholds.  \n- HYPITAT-II (Broekhuijsen et al., Lancet 2015) informed timing-of-birth decisions for 34\u201337 weeks.  \n- Churchill D, Duley L. (Cochrane 2002/2013) \u2014 interventionist vs expectant care for severe pre-eclampsia before term.  \n- Multiple RCTs compared antihypertensives: Vermillion 1999; Fenakel 1991; Aali 2002; Elatrous 2002; Vigil-De Gracia 2006/2013; Sibai 1987/1992; others listed in references.\n\nLimitations highlighted\n- Overall low-to-very-low quality evidence for many critical outcomes; trials generally small and heterogeneous; selection bias in observational studies; subgroup analyses by country income reflect country-level differences, not intra-UK setting differences.\n- No economic evaluations specific to this question identified.\n\nDisclaimer\n- Recommendations summarized reflect NICE committee views based on evidence available to Feb 2018; clinical judgement should be applied in individual cases.",
