{
  "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7061357/",
  "title": "Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre\u2010eclampsia",
  "content": "Topic: Antenatal abdominal decompression for maternal hypertension or impaired fetal growth \u2014 Cochrane review (updated search 2 Feb 2012)\n\nIntervention\n- Abdominal decompression: rigid dome + airtight suit around the abdomen; negative pressure \u221250 to \u2212100 mmHg applied for 15\u201330 seconds each minute for 30 minutes once to thrice daily, or continuously during labour. Proposed mechanism: increased intervillous blood flow.\n\nScope and trials\n- Objective: assess effects of antenatal abdominal decompression for pre\u2011eclampsia and/or suspected fetal compromise on fetal growth and perinatal outcomes.\n- Trials identified: 3 randomized or quasi\u2011randomized trials reported 1967\u20131973:\n  - Blecher 1967 (pre\u2011eclampsia, essential hypertension, chronic nephritis)\n  - MacRae 1971 (small\u2011for\u2011date fetuses, low urinary estriol)\n  - Varma 1973 (clinically small\u2011for\u2011dates, biparietal diameter <10th percentile)\n- Total sample reported in text: 356 pregnant women (review notes some inconsistencies; perinatal mortality analysis cites 367 participants).\n- One excluded non\u2011randomized trial: Coppola 1985.\n\nKey quantitative results (effect sizes reported as Risk Ratio [RR] with 95% CI; number of studies/participants shown in review table)\n- Persistent (unchanged or worsening) pre\u2011eclampsia: 1 study, 80 participants \u2014 RR 0.36 (95% CI 0.18 to 0.72).\n- Fetal distress in labour: 1 study, 140 participants \u2014 RR 0.37 (95% CI 0.19 to 0.71).\n- Low birthweight: 2 studies, 304 participants \u2014 RR 0.50 (95% CI 0.40 to 0.63).\n- Apgar score <6 at 1 minute: 1 study, 140 participants \u2014 RR 0.26 (95% CI 0.12 to 0.56).\n- Perinatal mortality: 3 studies, 367 participants \u2014 RR 0.39 (95% CI 0.22 to 0.71).\n- Induction of labour (all indications): 2 studies, 300 participants \u2014 RR 0.97 (95% CI 0.75 to 1.25).\n- Induction for placental insufficiency: 1 study, 140 participants \u2014 RR 0.74 (95% CI 0.46 to 1.19).\n- Endocrine / growth measures (Varma 1973):\n  - Urinary estriol increase: mean 1.68 (SD 1.21) vs 0.91 (SD 0.74) mg/week (decompression vs control).\n  - Weekly fetal biparietal diameter growth: mean 2.08 (SD 0.36) vs 1.49 (SD 0.71) mm/week.\n- MacRae 1971 reported increased mean birthweight and placental weight (data summarized qualitatively).\n\nQuality and bias\n- All three included studies have substantial methodological limitations:\n  - Allocation by alternation or unspecified \"at random\" (high risk of selection bias).\n  - Lack of observer blinding; some outcome assessments subjective.\n  - In Varma 1973 seven women in each group were reallocated due to non\u2011acceptance of decompression; inability to reanalyse by original allocation.\n  - Potential selection, observer, reporting and analysis biases.\n- Because of these limitations, observed effects may be exaggerated or spurious for subjective outcomes; birthweight is less susceptible to observer bias.\n\nAuthors\u2019 conclusions and implications\n- Clinical use of antenatal abdominal decompression cannot be recommended on the basis of these trials due to methodological shortcomings.\n- The consistent and large apparent improvements in birthweight and reductions in perinatal mortality across trials are striking and justify further evaluation in well\u2011designed randomized controlled trials, particularly for impaired fetal growth and possibly for pre\u2011eclampsia.\n\nOther review details\n- Review searches: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group\u2019s Trials Register (search date 2 Feb 2012); no new eligible trials identified in that update.\n- Review history: protocol published 1996; review first published 1996; subsequent updates (notably 2004, 2008, 2012).",
