2026-07-26 12:38:43,907 - sciconharness.mcp_client.utils.utils - INFO - Content: [[[Based on a Cochrane systematic review (Hofmeyr 2012) that pooled three small randomized trials from 1967–1973 (total N≈356) in pregnant women with hypertension and/or suspected fetal compromise, antenatal abdominal decompression was associated with statistically significant reductions in persistent pre-eclampsia (RR 0.36, 95% CI 0.18–0.72), fetal distress in labour (RR 0.37, 95% CI 0.19–0.71), low birthweight (RR 0.50, 95% CI 0.40–0.63), low Apgar scores at one minute (RR 0.26, 95% CI 0.12–0.56), and perinatal mortality (RR 0.39, 95% CI 0.22–0.71). However, all three included trials have serious methodological shortcomings—quasi-random allocation (e.g., alternation), lack of blinding, potential selection and reporting biases, and very small sample sizes—which substantially undermine confidence in these effect estimates. The striking apparent benefits on birthweight and perinatal mortality must therefore be interpreted with considerable caution. No new trials have been reported in th...
